1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,400

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,600
Today I'll talk about
advancing robot intelligence

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,600
with reinforcement learning.

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:17,220
And before I zoom into robotics
or reinforcement learning,

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:19,120
I'll take a step back.

6
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:22,520
And I'll first contrast
reinforcement learning

7
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:25,280
with imitation learning.

8
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:28,120
So one thing to note
here is that I'm

9
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:30,240
going to compare
imitation learning that

10
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:32,560
actually works with
reinforcement learning that

11
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:34,040
actually works.

12
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:36,280
That doesn't mean that they
don't exist reinforcement

13
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:38,580
learning algorithms which don't
follow this characteristics,

14
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:39,955
but these are the
characteristics

15
00:00:39,955 --> 00:00:42,400
of the ones that have
been the most successful

16
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:45,840
at actually solving
extremely hard problems.

17
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:47,760
So for imitation
learning, it is widely

18
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,540
used for pre-training
vision language models.

19
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:54,160
It's also used for video
generation image segmentation.

20
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:56,692
So the first two are
probably more popular.

21
00:00:56,692 --> 00:00:58,400
For image segmentation,
you get an image.

22
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:01,877
And it will segment out
different parts of the image.

23
00:01:01,877 --> 00:01:03,710
The best models that
we have today for these

24
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:06,630
are trained using some
form of imitation learning.

25
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:08,710
And reinforcement
learning, the places

26
00:01:08,710 --> 00:01:12,390
where it has delivered the best
results, one is game playing.

27
00:01:12,390 --> 00:01:16,910
So for example, the game of
Go, also games in simulation.

28
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:20,670
It has also delivered
results in reasoning

29
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:22,750
for large language models,
which is significantly

30
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:25,230
beyond what the imitation
learning pre-training could

31
00:01:25,230 --> 00:01:26,270
achieve.

32
00:01:26,270 --> 00:01:28,150
And the last one is
robotics, which I will

33
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:30,830
get into the rest of the talk.

34
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:33,670
So the key characteristics that
successful imitation learning

35
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:36,810
has used is that it
relies on off-policy data.

36
00:01:36,810 --> 00:01:40,093
So this data is collected
or generated by humans

37
00:01:40,093 --> 00:01:41,510
or collected in
sources which have

38
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:44,470
nothing to do with the final
policy that you're improving.

39
00:01:44,470 --> 00:01:47,470
In contrast, the reinforcement
learning that has succeeded

40
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:49,370
is typically on policy.

41
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:51,390
So the policy that
you want to improve

42
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:52,982
is the one you roll
out to generate

43
00:01:52,982 --> 00:01:55,190
the distribution of the data
set and then you somehow

44
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:58,175
use to improve your policy.

45
00:01:58,175 --> 00:02:00,800
In imitation learning, you take
your data set, you clean it up,

46
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:03,220
you get the good
data set, and then

47
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,860
you use that to
train your model.

48
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:08,580
But in reinforcement
learning, every data point

49
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:11,500
that is generated during the
policy rollout is fair game.

50
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:14,080
So you can use all of that to
improve and update your policy.

51
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:17,320
And that actually is one of the
things that makes reinforcement

52
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:18,320
learning quite powerful.

53
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:20,780
So you learn from not
just the positive but also

54
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:26,060
the negative data, which gives
you very precise behaviors

55
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:27,540
that you want.

56
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:30,740
And typically,
imitation learning

57
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:32,563
has given us generalists.

58
00:02:32,563 --> 00:02:33,980
It gives you this
one single model

59
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:36,220
that can do a bunch of things.

60
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:39,660
Reinforcement learning
either gives specialists

61
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:43,540
or it helps you specialize
pre-trained general models.

62
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,650
So this contrast
is just to give you

63
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:47,400
a sense of the difference
between the two.

64
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:49,983
That is not to say that one is
strictly better than the other.

65
00:02:49,983 --> 00:02:53,980
So even for the game
of Go, there were--

66
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,500
we use imitation
learning for warm

67
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:58,500
starting the reinforcement
learning self-play or search.

68
00:02:58,500 --> 00:02:59,893
And same with language models.

69
00:02:59,893 --> 00:03:03,102
So you use imitation
learning to warm-start

70
00:03:03,102 --> 00:03:05,060
your whole model and then
you can get reasoning

71
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:08,500
or whatever it is that
you want to improve

72
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,460
the performance on top of that.

73
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:16,340
So now, let's zoom into
reinforcement learning.

74
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:19,660
And the first question is,
what has it actually achieved?

75
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:22,740
Qualitatively, what
behaviors has it achieved,

76
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:26,340
which is the only way
to do those things?

77
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:28,900
So for AlphaGo, it learned to--

78
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:30,962
AlphaGo, it's a game of Go.

79
00:03:30,962 --> 00:03:32,420
And we have a system
called AlphaGo

80
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:35,940
which can defeat the best
Go player in the world.

81
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:38,020
And what it has learned
to do is to exceed

82
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:42,260
what humans can do by figuring
out innovative or new behaviors

83
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:45,340
that humans have
not seen before.

84
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:47,120
Similarly, for
reasoning and LLMs,

85
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,060
it can go significantly
beyond what

86
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:51,380
you can achieve with
just imitation learning,

87
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:54,340
and it can give you results just
significantly better than what

88
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:57,350
you would get otherwise.

89
00:03:57,350 --> 00:03:59,050
The key thing that
it shows here is

90
00:03:59,050 --> 00:04:01,330
very coherent and
precise reasoning

91
00:04:01,330 --> 00:04:03,075
over very long horizon tasks.

92
00:04:03,075 --> 00:04:04,450
So this is another
characteristic

93
00:04:04,450 --> 00:04:07,130
that we know that reinforcement
learning can give us.

94
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:09,550
And the last thing is
dexterity in robotics,

95
00:04:09,550 --> 00:04:13,730
which I'll get to soon.

96
00:04:13,730 --> 00:04:15,970
So if you look at
all three of these,

97
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:19,690
there's one question that, what
is common between all three

98
00:04:19,690 --> 00:04:24,050
of these which lets us achieve
the scale of results we

99
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:26,250
do with reinforcement learning?

100
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:29,290
So I would like to point
out two things which

101
00:04:29,290 --> 00:04:32,490
let us on-policy reinforcement
learning shine in all three

102
00:04:32,490 --> 00:04:33,810
of these domains.

103
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:35,710
The first is
well-specified rewards.

104
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:39,720
So for AlphaGo, the reward
was a very sparse one.

105
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:40,470
It was very clear.

106
00:04:40,470 --> 00:04:41,650
If you won the
game of Go, you can

107
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:43,733
look at the state of the
world, and you can define

108
00:04:43,733 --> 00:04:45,650
if you won the game or not.

109
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:47,390
Similarly for
reasoning and LLMs,

110
00:04:47,390 --> 00:04:50,710
if we look at this
DeepSeek carbon paper,

111
00:04:50,710 --> 00:04:53,840
they say that we use
a rule-based reward

112
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:55,700
function, which is
very clear to evaluate.

113
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:57,908
And now, since it is
rule-based, you can automate it.

114
00:04:57,908 --> 00:05:00,960
And you can get it very
cleanly and clearly at scale.

115
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:03,600
And the second
critical component

116
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:06,960
is, can we run these
policies at scale?

117
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:09,700
So with AlphaGo, since we
know the rules of the games,

118
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,300
we can put that
game in simulation.

119
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:13,180
And now it scales
beautifully with compute.

120
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:14,860
So we can just
throw compute at it.

121
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:16,600
You can evaluate the policies.

122
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:19,740
And we can convert
that compute to data,

123
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:21,600
which we will then
use reinforcement

124
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:24,040
learning to improve the model.

125
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:27,560
Similarly for reasoning in
LLMs, you can just roll them out

126
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:30,000
in a cluster and
you can evaluate

127
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,820
them using some form of an
automatic reward function,

128
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:36,455
making this an extremely
scalable way of collecting data

129
00:05:36,455 --> 00:05:38,580
for reinforcement learning,
and also evaluating it,

130
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:39,960
and then improving it.

131
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:43,640
So I would argue that
these two things are--

132
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:48,280
all of these results happened
seven to eight years--

133
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:50,200
spaced out by seven
to eight years.

134
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:52,750
And all of these
successes still have

135
00:05:52,750 --> 00:05:56,950
these very two key ingredients
which lets it succeed.

136
00:05:56,950 --> 00:05:59,670
So now, I'll get into--

137
00:05:59,670 --> 00:06:03,830
zoom into robotics with a focus
on what these ingredients are.

138
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:08,550
So if you zoom into robotics,
the results I'll present

139
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:10,090
are from three, four years ago.

140
00:06:10,090 --> 00:06:13,430
And the question is what
let us succeed in robotics

141
00:06:13,430 --> 00:06:15,310
with the same algorithm?

142
00:06:15,310 --> 00:06:19,270
So the closest approximation
to getting these ingredients

143
00:06:19,270 --> 00:06:20,950
is to use simulation.

144
00:06:20,950 --> 00:06:23,830
So if we use simulation,
we can programmatically

145
00:06:23,830 --> 00:06:26,630
calculate rewards because we
have full state of the world.

146
00:06:26,630 --> 00:06:28,550
And we can also evaluate
policies in scale

147
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:30,510
because we have converted
a physical problem

148
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:35,270
to a digital problem, which now
just will scale with compute.

149
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:38,997
So this gets us to solving
robotics and simulation.

150
00:06:38,997 --> 00:06:40,830
But that is not the
same as solving robotics

151
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:42,030
in the real world.

152
00:06:42,030 --> 00:06:45,750
So how do we get robotics
in the real world?

153
00:06:45,750 --> 00:06:47,770
By just tackling the
sim to real challenge.

154
00:06:47,770 --> 00:06:50,090
So how do we train
policies and simulation?

155
00:06:50,090 --> 00:06:52,780
And then how do we move
them to the real world?

156
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:54,520
And that will be the
focus of this talk.

157
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:57,620

158
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:02,160
Before I get into how we solve
these sim to real problem,

159
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:05,600
let me convince you first
that this actually works.

160
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:07,900
So I'll start with the
results, real-world results

161
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:10,960
of robots in the wild.

162
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:13,220
So this is in Berkeley.

163
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:14,980
And the robot is--

164
00:07:14,980 --> 00:07:17,180
everything is
real-time on board.

165
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:20,180
The robot is blind, and it's
trying to get over these rocks

166
00:07:20,180 --> 00:07:21,720
and the feet get stuck.

167
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:23,980
It keeps trying and
then figures out

168
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:27,940
that there's something and
then continues to walk.

169
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:32,080
Here it has these staircases
that it cannot see.

170
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:34,520
So it's a bit fumbly
in these staircases,

171
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:36,480
but it can still get
them and doesn't fall.

172
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:39,740

173
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:41,933
He also tried on
this grass patch.

174
00:07:41,933 --> 00:07:43,600
Even if you had vision
in this scenario,

175
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:45,120
you would not see the
ground underneath.

176
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:46,828
So you need a certain
level of robustness

177
00:07:46,828 --> 00:07:48,820
just to get through
some of these.

178
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:52,790
And here the ground is
sloping to the left.

179
00:07:52,790 --> 00:07:55,490
I think there's a big
ditch on the left.

180
00:07:55,490 --> 00:07:57,530
It still manages to not
just drift all the way

181
00:07:57,530 --> 00:07:59,770
and counter that.

182
00:07:59,770 --> 00:08:01,850
This is a construction
site in front of my house

183
00:08:01,850 --> 00:08:03,330
when I was in Berkeley.

184
00:08:03,330 --> 00:08:05,210
And we were able to repeat this.

185
00:08:05,210 --> 00:08:09,450
And it can reliably get down
this very unstable ground.

186
00:08:09,450 --> 00:08:12,330
And here it's slightly muddy,
so the feet get stuck once

187
00:08:12,330 --> 00:08:14,258
in a while, but it
knows how to correct

188
00:08:14,258 --> 00:08:16,050
for it by applying more
force and then just

189
00:08:16,050 --> 00:08:18,930
like getting out of it.

190
00:08:18,930 --> 00:08:22,760
And this is just
walking on sand.

191
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:25,370

192
00:08:25,370 --> 00:08:28,050
So the important point here
is that all of these results

193
00:08:28,050 --> 00:08:30,190
are from the exact same policy.

194
00:08:30,190 --> 00:08:33,210
So the exact same weights
deployed zero shot

195
00:08:33,210 --> 00:08:38,450
in the real world without any
tuning or specific improvement

196
00:08:38,450 --> 00:08:41,409
related to any of
these terrains.

197
00:08:41,409 --> 00:08:44,010
And the question is,
how do we get it?

198
00:08:44,010 --> 00:08:45,430
So that is the
focus of this work.

199
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:50,130
This was published
in RSS in 2021.

200
00:08:50,130 --> 00:08:52,830
So let's start by learning
to walk in simulation.

201
00:08:52,830 --> 00:08:54,310
So the idea is simple.

202
00:08:54,310 --> 00:08:56,710
Let's just pretend that this
simulation is the real world

203
00:08:56,710 --> 00:08:58,930
and everything we can
access is fair game.

204
00:08:58,930 --> 00:09:00,510
So we want to train
a neural network.

205
00:09:00,510 --> 00:09:02,010
That's your base policy.

206
00:09:02,010 --> 00:09:03,870
It will take an
action in simulation.

207
00:09:03,870 --> 00:09:07,988
Simulation will simulate
one tick of the real world,

208
00:09:07,988 --> 00:09:09,530
and then it will
give you the updated

209
00:09:09,530 --> 00:09:11,780
state of the simulation,
which the policy can consume,

210
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:14,090
and then it can walk.

211
00:09:14,090 --> 00:09:16,010
So this would be a
very narrow policy

212
00:09:16,010 --> 00:09:17,350
that can walk in one scenario.

213
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:19,930
And maybe we can deploy
it in the real world

214
00:09:19,930 --> 00:09:23,150
by tuning some parameters
of the physics.

215
00:09:23,150 --> 00:09:26,970
But this is not good
enough because we actually

216
00:09:26,970 --> 00:09:28,430
don't want a single policy.

217
00:09:28,430 --> 00:09:30,250
We want a generalist
policy that can

218
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:32,530
walk in all of these scenarios.

219
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:36,770
So we will randomize all of
these parameters in simulation.

220
00:09:36,770 --> 00:09:41,570
And the important part is we
will let the policy have access

221
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:43,730
to all of these parameters.

222
00:09:43,730 --> 00:09:45,210
So now when we
train this policy,

223
00:09:45,210 --> 00:09:47,640
it can walk, not just
for any specific value

224
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:49,237
of mass or friction.

225
00:09:49,237 --> 00:09:50,820
It would have seen
a huge range of it,

226
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:52,620
and it will know how
to walk accordingly.

227
00:09:52,620 --> 00:09:54,940
So it's not oblivious or
blind to the environment.

228
00:09:54,940 --> 00:09:57,080
It's actually aware of
what it's dealing with.

229
00:09:57,080 --> 00:09:59,060
And for the purposes
of this talk,

230
00:09:59,060 --> 00:10:03,240
we'll call this compressed
vector extrinsics.

231
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:05,600
And then the reward
function we use

232
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,615
is to track a certain
target velocity, and then

233
00:10:08,615 --> 00:10:10,240
a certain other set
of reward functions

234
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:12,437
which minimize work
and ground impact.

235
00:10:12,437 --> 00:10:14,520
We'll train this whole
system end-to-end with PPO.

236
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:17,580
Takes about a billion samples
to train it in this simulation.

237
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:21,560
This is [? resin. ?]
And once we have this,

238
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:23,858
now we can think about
how to deploy it.

239
00:10:23,858 --> 00:10:26,400
But before that, let's maybe
zoom in into the reward function

240
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:27,640
that we have.

241
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:30,640
So we have 10 different
reward functions.

242
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:32,720
This is not very
elegant, but we tried

243
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:34,060
to be as principled as we can.

244
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:36,400
So the forward and the
latter-- so the first reward

245
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:37,900
function is just
specify the task.

246
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:39,883
So what is the task that
we're trying to solve?

247
00:10:39,883 --> 00:10:41,800
And then there are a
bunch of reward functions

248
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,120
which minimize energetics.

249
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:46,870
And then the last one is
stability and minimizing

250
00:10:46,870 --> 00:10:48,990
hardware damage, because
in simulation there's

251
00:10:48,990 --> 00:10:50,250
no concept of hardware damage.

252
00:10:50,250 --> 00:10:52,590
So we need to inject
all of those biases

253
00:10:52,590 --> 00:10:54,710
so that it can learn
something which can actually

254
00:10:54,710 --> 00:10:56,010
transfer to the real world.

255
00:10:56,010 --> 00:10:59,070

256
00:10:59,070 --> 00:11:01,350
So coming back to how
can we deploy it--

257
00:11:01,350 --> 00:11:04,510
well, this vector is
unknown in the real world.

258
00:11:04,510 --> 00:11:08,710
And even if I estimated
that in my living room,

259
00:11:08,710 --> 00:11:12,230
it would still not be
enough, because the moment

260
00:11:12,230 --> 00:11:14,325
I get to something which
is not my living room,

261
00:11:14,325 --> 00:11:16,950
let's say sand or mulch or any
of those, it would fail to work,

262
00:11:16,950 --> 00:11:19,070
and I have to retune it again.

263
00:11:19,070 --> 00:11:23,750
So the key idea that
we will use here

264
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:26,510
is we will estimate
this online in fractions

265
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:29,590
of a second at runtime.

266
00:11:29,590 --> 00:11:32,590
And the reason why
we can estimate it

267
00:11:32,590 --> 00:11:37,070
at all in the
first place is by--

268
00:11:37,070 --> 00:11:38,910
the way we will
estimate-- the signal that

269
00:11:38,910 --> 00:11:42,170
will be used to estimate it
is the observation history,

270
00:11:42,170 --> 00:11:44,900
so the series of commands
that are executed

271
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:46,860
and the commands that
were actually issued.

272
00:11:46,860 --> 00:11:49,100
We will use that as our
input into the system

273
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:50,900
to estimate this online.

274
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:56,020
And the reason this signal has
the right thing that we care

275
00:11:56,020 --> 00:11:58,240
about is-- let's say that
you give a command to walk,

276
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:00,100
and then one of your
leg slips more than

277
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:01,780
what you expected it to.

278
00:12:01,780 --> 00:12:03,935
You're at the discrepancy
between your signal

279
00:12:03,935 --> 00:12:06,060
and what you actually
[? commanded ?] will tell you

280
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:07,198
that you actually slipped.

281
00:12:07,198 --> 00:12:08,240
This was not intentional.

282
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:09,540
You slipped forward.

283
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:12,340
So the idea is to use the
history of this signal

284
00:12:12,340 --> 00:12:14,860
to estimate what
these extrinsics are.

285
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:17,860
And then this discrepancy
between the actual movement

286
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:20,260
and the measured
movement is what

287
00:12:20,260 --> 00:12:22,860
will let us estimate
this online at runtime

288
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:24,820
in fractions of a second.

289
00:12:24,820 --> 00:12:27,580
So the next question
is, how do we

290
00:12:27,580 --> 00:12:31,260
train this model so that we can
deploy it in the real world?

291
00:12:31,260 --> 00:12:33,560
And the idea is simple.

292
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:35,260
We'll just go back
to simulation.

293
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:37,280
And in simulation, we
have the ground truth.

294
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:39,060
And we also have the history.

295
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:43,290
So we will use dagger in which
you roll out the student policy

296
00:12:43,290 --> 00:12:45,150
to generate the
data distribution,

297
00:12:45,150 --> 00:12:47,690
and then you use the teacher
to supervise every single point

298
00:12:47,690 --> 00:12:50,050
of the student policy.

299
00:12:50,050 --> 00:12:54,050
You will use that to train the
student model, which is now

300
00:12:54,050 --> 00:12:57,010
using this history
of observations

301
00:12:57,010 --> 00:13:00,670
to continuously estimate
what this extrinsic z is,

302
00:13:00,670 --> 00:13:03,650
which will then be fed into the
base policy which is trained

303
00:13:03,650 --> 00:13:04,830
with reinforcement learning.

304
00:13:04,830 --> 00:13:08,130
And we copied over the weights.

305
00:13:08,130 --> 00:13:10,170
And then we can now
deploy this online.

306
00:13:10,170 --> 00:13:14,290
So one thing to note
here is, the base policy

307
00:13:14,290 --> 00:13:15,470
runs at 100 Hertz.

308
00:13:15,470 --> 00:13:17,090
And the adaptation
module, which is

309
00:13:17,090 --> 00:13:19,250
the one that estimates
this extrinsics,

310
00:13:19,250 --> 00:13:21,110
that only runs at 10 Hertz.

311
00:13:21,110 --> 00:13:23,150
And this is not by choice.

312
00:13:23,150 --> 00:13:25,515
It's because of whatever
compute was available on board.

313
00:13:25,515 --> 00:13:27,390
And we see that the
performance doesn't drop.

314
00:13:27,390 --> 00:13:29,550
So this was not accounted
for during training.

315
00:13:29,550 --> 00:13:30,967
So in training we
train everything

316
00:13:30,967 --> 00:13:32,730
at 100 Hertz in lockstep.

317
00:13:32,730 --> 00:13:35,610
And once we deploy it,
we see that this can only

318
00:13:35,610 --> 00:13:37,810
run at 10 Hertz, and
this runs at 100 Hertz.

319
00:13:37,810 --> 00:13:40,760
And that is good enough to
get the results that we want.

320
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:44,340
So let's start with
evaluating it indoors.

321
00:13:44,340 --> 00:13:47,440
This is my living room, and
I'll pour some oil in it.

322
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:49,110
This is a plastic tarp.

323
00:13:49,110 --> 00:13:51,640

324
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:56,160
I think that is olive oil.

325
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:57,300
This is a while ago.

326
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:00,300
So this a plastic tarp
on which I evaluated.

327
00:14:00,300 --> 00:14:02,880
And then the robot's feet also
have plastic covered on top

328
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:04,900
of it, partially to
make it challenging,

329
00:14:04,900 --> 00:14:08,040
but also to protect
the feet of the robot.

330
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:12,600
And it slips a bit here, but
it continues to march forward

331
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:15,192
without an issue.

332
00:14:15,192 --> 00:14:16,400
You can slow it down and see.

333
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:18,560
This is where it
starts slipping,

334
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:22,240
and it seems to maintain
balance and not flip over.

335
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:28,040
Similarly, here I'm throwing
a 5 Kg payload on the robot,

336
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:30,100
and it seems to be doing fine.

337
00:14:30,100 --> 00:14:32,280
Just to be clear,
this robot is 12 Kgs.

338
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:35,880
5 Kgs is roughly half
the weight of the robot.

339
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:39,840
I can make this a bit slower.

340
00:14:39,840 --> 00:14:42,220
And you see what
actually goes down

341
00:14:42,220 --> 00:14:43,560
if you focus on the green dots.

342
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:45,360
In a few steps, it
tries to go back up

343
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:50,840
and recover the regular motion
style that it is working with.

344
00:14:50,840 --> 00:14:54,740
Similarly, here, it's like the
foothold is not even stable.

345
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:56,540
So this plank just
moves with the foot.

346
00:14:56,540 --> 00:14:59,600
So there's no clear estimate
of the ground that you can get.

347
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:00,580
And it's still walking.

348
00:15:00,580 --> 00:15:02,060
All of these results
are blind by the way.

349
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:02,940
There's no vision.

350
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:07,320
All of these just use
proprioception history.

351
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:11,200
And I evaluated it on a bunch of
places, including my mattress,

352
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:14,840
on foam, taking a step down,
step up, and all of these.

353
00:15:14,840 --> 00:15:19,440
And it was relatively robust
in all of these scenarios.

354
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:21,880
So let's see what happens if
we switch up this adaptation

355
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:22,380
module.

356
00:15:22,380 --> 00:15:25,200
So what this means is we
estimate the extrinsics

357
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:26,560
at the first time step.

358
00:15:26,560 --> 00:15:27,975
And then we freeze that update.

359
00:15:27,975 --> 00:15:29,600
And then we don't
update the extrinsics

360
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:31,200
for the rest of the run.

361
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:32,380
And it looks like it is--

362
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:32,880
8 Kg.

363
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:35,420
This is like a 12 Kg robot,
and 8 Kg is very high.

364
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:38,070
And you can see the robot
is struggling a bit.

365
00:15:38,070 --> 00:15:40,310
So the rated payload
for this robot

366
00:15:40,310 --> 00:15:44,230
when I bought it was
around 3 kilograms.

367
00:15:44,230 --> 00:15:47,450
Maxing this out heats
the motors much faster,

368
00:15:47,450 --> 00:15:49,050
but it can still manage to walk.

369
00:15:49,050 --> 00:15:50,800
If you switch off this
adaptation module--

370
00:15:50,800 --> 00:15:54,150

371
00:15:54,150 --> 00:15:55,990
So the weight actually
tilts the bot forward

372
00:15:55,990 --> 00:15:58,750
and it doesn't know to correct
for it by applying more force

373
00:15:58,750 --> 00:16:02,248
or torque in the front legs or
get back to a higher clearance

374
00:16:02,248 --> 00:16:03,790
and just ends up
tilting forward when

375
00:16:03,790 --> 00:16:09,110
it doesn't have enough torque
to clear for the next step.

376
00:16:09,110 --> 00:16:11,870
Same thing with the foam.

377
00:16:11,870 --> 00:16:16,670
Even if it is getting stuck,
it seems to manage that fine.

378
00:16:16,670 --> 00:16:19,812
But here, it doesn't know
to again apply more force

379
00:16:19,812 --> 00:16:21,270
to get higher
clearance to continue

380
00:16:21,270 --> 00:16:22,687
walking because
it hasn't detected

381
00:16:22,687 --> 00:16:26,150
that event or that change.

382
00:16:26,150 --> 00:16:30,470
So let's zoom into what is this
adaptation module doing exactly.

383
00:16:30,470 --> 00:16:33,570
So this is the plastic surface
that I showed you before.

384
00:16:33,570 --> 00:16:36,420
It's flipped.

385
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:38,860
And there are three
plots that I have here.

386
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:41,920
So the top plot is what
is called a gait plot.

387
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:43,920
So these are the four
feet of the robot.

388
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:46,500
And anytime a line
is solid, it means

389
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:48,540
that the feet is in contact.

390
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:52,060
There are feet sensors in the
hardware which let us plot this.

391
00:16:52,060 --> 00:16:54,300
The second plot is
the amount of torque

392
00:16:54,300 --> 00:17:00,460
being requested in the front
right knee of the robot.

393
00:17:00,460 --> 00:17:03,420
And Z1 and Z5 are
two values that we

394
00:17:03,420 --> 00:17:05,513
have taken from the
adaptation module,

395
00:17:05,513 --> 00:17:06,680
and we've just plotted them.

396
00:17:06,680 --> 00:17:09,900
I chose these two because
they show the most variation

397
00:17:09,900 --> 00:17:12,900
in the course of this run.

398
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:14,720
So if you see that
the moment it slips,

399
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:17,420
there's some
shuffling in the gait.

400
00:17:17,420 --> 00:17:21,358
We also see a variation
in the torque profile.

401
00:17:21,358 --> 00:17:22,900
And we see that the
adaptation module

402
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:25,319
changes its value to capture
that something has happened.

403
00:17:25,319 --> 00:17:27,400
Since these are
learned extrinsics,

404
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:30,120
we cannot interpret
them very easily,

405
00:17:30,120 --> 00:17:32,300
but we see that some
change has happened.

406
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:35,130
And after that, it seems to
have recovered to the same gait

407
00:17:35,130 --> 00:17:36,730
pattern and a similar
torque profile

408
00:17:36,730 --> 00:17:42,170
as before once the event is
detected in the bottom graph.

409
00:17:42,170 --> 00:17:46,466
Similarly, for this
example, there's a--

410
00:17:46,466 --> 00:17:48,365
the 5 Kg payload,
which is dropped,

411
00:17:48,365 --> 00:17:49,990
which means that at
the time of impact,

412
00:17:49,990 --> 00:17:51,930
the force is probably even more.

413
00:17:51,930 --> 00:17:53,310
And we can do the same test.

414
00:17:53,310 --> 00:17:55,768
And we see that there's, again,
the shuffling of this gait.

415
00:17:55,768 --> 00:17:59,010
And the torque is very high
at the moment when this drops,

416
00:17:59,010 --> 00:18:00,950
but it comes back
to a regular gait.

417
00:18:00,950 --> 00:18:03,930
But the torque that it
saturates to after it tries to--

418
00:18:03,930 --> 00:18:06,790
after it is done with
adapting to this event,

419
00:18:06,790 --> 00:18:09,290
the torque it saturates
to is higher than

420
00:18:09,290 --> 00:18:10,875
before because it
needs more torque

421
00:18:10,875 --> 00:18:12,500
to balance the way
that it is carrying.

422
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:15,590

423
00:18:15,590 --> 00:18:19,170
So let's get into some
quantitative comparison.

424
00:18:19,170 --> 00:18:22,490
The first baseline is a very
simple change over this.

425
00:18:22,490 --> 00:18:25,810
It's just train a policy
without the knowledge

426
00:18:25,810 --> 00:18:27,473
of changing environments.

427
00:18:27,473 --> 00:18:28,890
You just trained
a policy which is

428
00:18:28,890 --> 00:18:31,650
blind and robust to every
single thing it can potentially

429
00:18:31,650 --> 00:18:32,470
encounter.

430
00:18:32,470 --> 00:18:35,343
This is a very common
technique which is used,

431
00:18:35,343 --> 00:18:36,760
which is just
domain randomization

432
00:18:36,760 --> 00:18:40,200
without necessarily caring about
if you are giving this input

433
00:18:40,200 --> 00:18:42,000
to the policy or not.

434
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,440
This is one of the baselines.

435
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:48,060
In the row above expert
is the RMA baseline.

436
00:18:48,060 --> 00:18:50,040
That's the method
that I just presented.

437
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:52,200
Stands for Rapid
Motor Adaptation.

438
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:53,920
And if you compare
that with robust,

439
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:55,700
we see that it is
a bit conservative,

440
00:18:55,700 --> 00:18:59,200
but it also has a
weaker success rate.

441
00:18:59,200 --> 00:19:02,080
And the amount of torque
it's exerting is also higher.

442
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:03,960
The amount of jerk
that it experiences

443
00:19:03,960 --> 00:19:06,940
is higher because it has no idea
of the environment around it.

444
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:09,560
It is just trying to be
as aggressive as possible

445
00:19:09,560 --> 00:19:12,200
to make sure that
it does not fall.

446
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:15,640
The second baseline we have
is for system identification.

447
00:19:15,640 --> 00:19:17,600
The goal for system
identification

448
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:21,145
is to estimate things
like friction and mass

449
00:19:21,145 --> 00:19:22,520
and all of these
other parameters

450
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:25,080
explicitly, and then
use them downstream.

451
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:27,720
So in this instantiation of
system identification, what

452
00:19:27,720 --> 00:19:29,880
we try to do is instead
of trying to predict

453
00:19:29,880 --> 00:19:32,480
the extrinsics, which
is a compressed version

454
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:35,080
of these environment
parameters, we directly

455
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:37,460
go for these SysID parameters.

456
00:19:37,460 --> 00:19:40,360
So we directly try to
predict friction and all

457
00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:43,640
of these other parameters.

458
00:19:43,640 --> 00:19:45,880
So this is a hard
problem, to predict

459
00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:48,760
exactly what the friction is,
because you might not even

460
00:19:48,760 --> 00:19:50,620
detect the full
scope of the slip,

461
00:19:50,620 --> 00:19:53,580
or you might not even know
exactly how much the mass is.

462
00:19:53,580 --> 00:19:56,560
But it's also not
necessary to solve

463
00:19:56,560 --> 00:19:59,585
for the final downstream
task of working robustly.

464
00:19:59,585 --> 00:20:00,960
And we see the
gap in performance

465
00:20:00,960 --> 00:20:04,920
if we try to explicitly
estimate these parameters.

466
00:20:04,920 --> 00:20:06,480
And interestingly,
it's lower than

467
00:20:06,480 --> 00:20:08,655
having a robust policy,
which is just agnostic to it

468
00:20:08,655 --> 00:20:11,280
and just learns a gait, which is
robust to all of these changes

469
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:13,240
to begin with.

470
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:16,680
The third baseline
is one that tries

471
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:18,460
to fine-tune at inference time.

472
00:20:18,460 --> 00:20:20,280
So you take a policy
from simulation.

473
00:20:20,280 --> 00:20:22,140
And to bridge the
sim to real gap,

474
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:24,300
you roll it out
in the real world,

475
00:20:24,300 --> 00:20:26,360
and then you improve the policy.

476
00:20:26,360 --> 00:20:28,640
This is one way
of achieving that.

477
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:31,190
The RMA style is
closer to in-context

478
00:20:31,190 --> 00:20:33,630
learning where you take
the history of everything,

479
00:20:33,630 --> 00:20:36,290
and then the learning happens
in the feedforward pass itself.

480
00:20:36,290 --> 00:20:38,050
So you see all of
these parameters.

481
00:20:38,050 --> 00:20:41,990
And then you estimate how
the network should behave,

482
00:20:41,990 --> 00:20:44,990
which lets it do it in
fractions of a second instead

483
00:20:44,990 --> 00:20:48,190
of trying to estimate
that at inference time

484
00:20:48,190 --> 00:20:51,110
by rolling out policies.

485
00:20:51,110 --> 00:20:53,810
And then the last baseline
is one with adaptation.

486
00:20:53,810 --> 00:20:57,010
So if you switch off adaptation,
the performance falls.

487
00:20:57,010 --> 00:20:59,530
We did this quantitatively,
qualitatively.

488
00:20:59,530 --> 00:21:02,305
And we have numbers in
the real world as well.

489
00:21:02,305 --> 00:21:04,930
We see that the performance is
lower than just using [? arm. ?]

490
00:21:04,930 --> 00:21:06,910
And there's a
small amount of gap

491
00:21:06,910 --> 00:21:08,750
between what we
get from the expert

492
00:21:08,750 --> 00:21:13,910
and from distilling it into an
RMA method which can actually

493
00:21:13,910 --> 00:21:16,336
be deployed.

494
00:21:16,336 --> 00:21:20,630
So everything that I've
shown up until now is blind.

495
00:21:20,630 --> 00:21:22,930
And the next thing
that I'll do--

496
00:21:22,930 --> 00:21:24,830
so if you want to
strive towards having

497
00:21:24,830 --> 00:21:28,310
some form of a
general robot model,

498
00:21:28,310 --> 00:21:30,380
it needs to have
perception or vision.

499
00:21:30,380 --> 00:21:35,820
And I will try to add vision to
what I just showed for walking.

500
00:21:35,820 --> 00:21:38,940
But we already saw all of
these results without vision.

501
00:21:38,940 --> 00:21:43,940
So the first question is, do we
even need vision for walking?

502
00:21:43,940 --> 00:21:44,982
Is it a good test?

503
00:21:44,982 --> 00:21:46,440
Do we even need
vision for walking?

504
00:21:46,440 --> 00:21:48,260
Can we not just do
without any vision

505
00:21:48,260 --> 00:21:51,820
altogether, given the
results we have already seen?

506
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:55,800
And the answer is that the
two scenarios, in one case,

507
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:56,720
vision is critical.

508
00:21:56,720 --> 00:21:59,740
If you are to cross these
gaps or stepping stones

509
00:21:59,740 --> 00:22:02,340
or make a jump, you
need vision to estimate

510
00:22:02,340 --> 00:22:03,940
how to make that jump.

511
00:22:03,940 --> 00:22:06,980
And the second argument is more
of an efficiency and elegance

512
00:22:06,980 --> 00:22:08,540
and longevity argument.

513
00:22:08,540 --> 00:22:10,460
So if you have to
collide with a staircase

514
00:22:10,460 --> 00:22:13,740
every single time that
you climb it, it's not--

515
00:22:13,740 --> 00:22:17,220
it's a very inelegant
policy that will--

516
00:22:17,220 --> 00:22:19,480
and the hardware will
probably not survive for long.

517
00:22:19,480 --> 00:22:22,500
So for both of these reasons, if
you have a vision in the loop,

518
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:24,220
you can potentially
get to results

519
00:22:24,220 --> 00:22:27,500
which are much better than what
we can achieve without vision.

520
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:30,150
And that will be the
focus of this work.

521
00:22:30,150 --> 00:22:33,970
This was published
[? and coded ?] 2022.

522
00:22:33,970 --> 00:22:36,410
So to repeat the
question, your question

523
00:22:36,410 --> 00:22:38,770
is, has there been
follow-up work

524
00:22:38,770 --> 00:22:42,130
done to use the rollouts
in the real world

525
00:22:42,130 --> 00:22:44,330
to improve the policy?

526
00:22:44,330 --> 00:22:46,850
So there are two parts
to this question.

527
00:22:46,850 --> 00:22:51,050
The one part is the sheer power
of using a general learning

528
00:22:51,050 --> 00:22:52,050
algorithm, which is--

529
00:22:52,050 --> 00:22:54,390
if you use reinforcement
learning, in the limit,

530
00:22:54,390 --> 00:22:57,170
you can learn whatever behavior
it is that you want to learn.

531
00:22:57,170 --> 00:22:59,410
And then there is
this argument of,

532
00:22:59,410 --> 00:23:02,610
if the policy makes a mistake
and figures out in a few tries

533
00:23:02,610 --> 00:23:06,050
how to fix it, will it
remember it in the future?

534
00:23:06,050 --> 00:23:09,590
So the first part, I
think, is an open problem,

535
00:23:09,590 --> 00:23:12,650
and we still don't have a
method which can continuously

536
00:23:12,650 --> 00:23:14,930
improve perpetually online.

537
00:23:14,930 --> 00:23:18,390
The second part of your
question is a simpler problem,

538
00:23:18,390 --> 00:23:20,290
which is correlate-- which is--

539
00:23:20,290 --> 00:23:23,250
where the simpler answer might
be to just increase the context

540
00:23:23,250 --> 00:23:25,290
that you give to the policy.

541
00:23:25,290 --> 00:23:29,240
So if you figure out a way to
compress the context of one day

542
00:23:29,240 --> 00:23:31,400
into the policy or two
days into the policy,

543
00:23:31,400 --> 00:23:33,200
you can imagine the
in-context learning

544
00:23:33,200 --> 00:23:37,400
to be sufficient to let it
remember how it fixed it

545
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:40,680
the last time and get it right
in one shot in the future.

546
00:23:40,680 --> 00:23:44,020
So there are two ways
to get to what you said.

547
00:23:44,020 --> 00:23:47,140
One of them is more powerful,
but also very slow to learn.

548
00:23:47,140 --> 00:23:49,920
And the other thing lets
you learn much faster

549
00:23:49,920 --> 00:23:51,260
in a much simpler way.

550
00:23:51,260 --> 00:23:55,740

551
00:23:55,740 --> 00:24:00,120
It's not obvious which
one is more complete.

552
00:24:00,120 --> 00:24:05,200
I can see a system which has
infinite context, like what

553
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:08,880
humans have, and is capable
of maintaining a memory, which

554
00:24:08,880 --> 00:24:12,160
lets us continuously improve
in the feedforward pass itself

555
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:13,525
without needing to take--

556
00:24:13,525 --> 00:24:15,400
without having to take
reinforcement learning

557
00:24:15,400 --> 00:24:16,720
gradients.

558
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:19,740
There's no reason
that cannot happen.

559
00:24:19,740 --> 00:24:21,200
But then the burden
of that shifts

560
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:22,612
to how do you train this model.

561
00:24:22,612 --> 00:24:24,320
So you need to do
something in simulation

562
00:24:24,320 --> 00:24:26,710
to get that behavior out of it.

563
00:24:26,710 --> 00:24:30,150
Yes, you can train directly
with phase 2 with more compute.

564
00:24:30,150 --> 00:24:32,255
I was using a
CPU-based simulation.

565
00:24:32,255 --> 00:24:33,630
[? With ?] a
GPU-based simulation

566
00:24:33,630 --> 00:24:35,870
with more GPUs,
that is possible.

567
00:24:35,870 --> 00:24:37,830
Most successful sim
to real policies

568
00:24:37,830 --> 00:24:43,030
are actually the ones
which don't have a history.

569
00:24:43,030 --> 00:24:45,830
They usually just use the
current state to deploy.

570
00:24:45,830 --> 00:24:49,990
And my guess for
why that is the case

571
00:24:49,990 --> 00:24:52,550
is because the moment you
have only the current state

572
00:24:52,550 --> 00:24:58,550
that you're using to deploy, it
becomes a state-indexed policy

573
00:24:58,550 --> 00:25:01,750
as opposed to a state
and time-indexed policy.

574
00:25:01,750 --> 00:25:06,090
So if you were to
have a history,

575
00:25:06,090 --> 00:25:08,630
you need to make sure that
the distribution you see

576
00:25:08,630 --> 00:25:12,830
in your history and you
randomize it sufficiently enough

577
00:25:12,830 --> 00:25:15,810
that when you go
into the real world,

578
00:25:15,810 --> 00:25:18,670
there's no exploitation that
happened during training, which

579
00:25:18,670 --> 00:25:20,270
is preventing it
from transferring

580
00:25:20,270 --> 00:25:22,090
as well to the real world.

581
00:25:22,090 --> 00:25:22,590
Sorry.

582
00:25:22,590 --> 00:25:24,370
To repeat the question--
the question was,

583
00:25:24,370 --> 00:25:26,550
can we just directly
train phase 2?

584
00:25:26,550 --> 00:25:28,592
And the answer is yes,
you can with more compute.

585
00:25:28,592 --> 00:25:30,050
But you need to be
careful with sim

586
00:25:30,050 --> 00:25:32,230
to real because the more
information you give it

587
00:25:32,230 --> 00:25:34,550
in sim, which is possible if
you have a very, very long

588
00:25:34,550 --> 00:25:37,750
and dense history, it might
learn to exploit things off

589
00:25:37,750 --> 00:25:39,630
either your
environment or physics

590
00:25:39,630 --> 00:25:41,950
in simulation or your
reward structure.

591
00:25:41,950 --> 00:25:43,350
And we do see this.

592
00:25:43,350 --> 00:25:47,510
If you give a periodic--

593
00:25:47,510 --> 00:25:50,550
if you give a periodic
push to the robot,

594
00:25:50,550 --> 00:25:52,630
it is very common for
the robot to just learn

595
00:25:52,630 --> 00:25:55,310
to be very robust when
it expects the push,

596
00:25:55,310 --> 00:25:56,810
when it has a long
enough context,

597
00:25:56,810 --> 00:25:58,510
or it has a notion of time.

598
00:25:58,510 --> 00:26:00,085
But when you randomize
that as well,

599
00:26:00,085 --> 00:26:01,210
then you can get around it.

600
00:26:01,210 --> 00:26:01,970
So it is possible.

601
00:26:01,970 --> 00:26:06,270
You just need to be more careful
with how you train your policy.

602
00:26:06,270 --> 00:26:10,830
So the physical
parameters in this is--

603
00:26:10,830 --> 00:26:14,370
there's a 2x compression here,
half of what the relative is.

604
00:26:14,370 --> 00:26:15,890
The extrinsics are around eight.

605
00:26:15,890 --> 00:26:20,190
I think the full physical
parameters are maybe 17 or 18.

606
00:26:20,190 --> 00:26:23,420
In this version of vision, there
will be a much more aggressive

607
00:26:23,420 --> 00:26:26,580
compression where it
goes from the full image

608
00:26:26,580 --> 00:26:28,900
to a much smaller encoder.

609
00:26:28,900 --> 00:26:31,660
The question is, in
the series of rewards

610
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:34,245
that we have, how do
we balance between all

611
00:26:34,245 --> 00:26:35,370
of these different rewards?

612
00:26:35,370 --> 00:26:38,740

613
00:26:38,740 --> 00:26:40,900
There's no good way to do it.

614
00:26:40,900 --> 00:26:42,320
That is the real answer.

615
00:26:42,320 --> 00:26:44,020
I think it's a
very hard problem.

616
00:26:44,020 --> 00:26:48,100
The practical way to do it is
to add one reward at a time,

617
00:26:48,100 --> 00:26:52,540
and then try to
see what is lacking

618
00:26:52,540 --> 00:26:54,460
in the current set of rewards.

619
00:26:54,460 --> 00:26:57,500
Add a new reward, and
then tune only that reward

620
00:26:57,500 --> 00:26:59,140
relative to every other reward.

621
00:26:59,140 --> 00:27:01,040
So instead of this
exponential complexity,

622
00:27:01,040 --> 00:27:06,060
you can reduce it somewhat
to just adding one at a time.

623
00:27:06,060 --> 00:27:08,460
But this is probably not--

624
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:11,380
the coefficients we get are
probably not the most optimal

625
00:27:11,380 --> 00:27:12,980
you can get.

626
00:27:12,980 --> 00:27:15,980
So the question is, in
input to the policy,

627
00:27:15,980 --> 00:27:18,532
we use the current state
and the previous action.

628
00:27:18,532 --> 00:27:20,240
What if we don't use
the previous action?

629
00:27:20,240 --> 00:27:24,633
So previous action has a very
important signal to give,

630
00:27:24,633 --> 00:27:27,050
which is, what is the command
that I give in the last time

631
00:27:27,050 --> 00:27:27,550
step?

632
00:27:27,550 --> 00:27:29,850
This lets you be
smooth, consistent,

633
00:27:29,850 --> 00:27:33,063
and it also lets you show some
amount of correction behavior.

634
00:27:33,063 --> 00:27:34,730
So if this action
didn't work and you're

635
00:27:34,730 --> 00:27:35,996
conditioned on that
action, you know

636
00:27:35,996 --> 00:27:37,930
that the next action
that you have to predict

637
00:27:37,930 --> 00:27:39,210
needs to be higher.

638
00:27:39,210 --> 00:27:41,570
So the state is lossy.

639
00:27:41,570 --> 00:27:43,790
If you remove the
previous action,

640
00:27:43,790 --> 00:27:47,410
my guess is that the
performance will drop compared

641
00:27:47,410 --> 00:27:49,950
to having the action as input.

642
00:27:49,950 --> 00:27:51,450
The question is,
what happens if you

643
00:27:51,450 --> 00:27:54,828
use two or three or more time
steps of the state history?

644
00:27:54,828 --> 00:27:56,370
That's related to
the other question,

645
00:27:56,370 --> 00:27:59,278
where what if we train the
full system end-to-end directly

646
00:27:59,278 --> 00:28:01,070
[? face ?] to using
reinforcement learning?

647
00:28:01,070 --> 00:28:02,060
You can do that.

648
00:28:02,060 --> 00:28:03,810
You just need to be a
bit careful with sim

649
00:28:03,810 --> 00:28:07,810
to real transfer, because now it
has more context and information

650
00:28:07,810 --> 00:28:12,110
to potentially exploit the
simulation or your environment

651
00:28:12,110 --> 00:28:17,010
or your reward structure in ways
which might not be desirable.

652
00:28:17,010 --> 00:28:18,290
The question is, what--

653
00:28:18,290 --> 00:28:20,760
to just quickly explain dagger.

654
00:28:20,760 --> 00:28:24,380
It's a very standard algorithm,
where what we do here,

655
00:28:24,380 --> 00:28:25,600
we apply that--

656
00:28:25,600 --> 00:28:31,100
the way we apply it here is
to run the phase 2 policy.

657
00:28:31,100 --> 00:28:34,160
So we initialize the adaptation
module, which is random weights.

658
00:28:34,160 --> 00:28:37,280
We run the phase 2
policy, collect the data.

659
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:39,080
Initially, it's just
going to be bad data

660
00:28:39,080 --> 00:28:42,080
because it hasn't learned
to do anything meaningful.

661
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:44,280
And then we'll use
the teacher encoder

662
00:28:44,280 --> 00:28:46,500
to supervise at every time step.

663
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:48,060
What should the
prediction have been?

664
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:49,560
And then we will
just use supervised

665
00:28:49,560 --> 00:28:52,480
learning to update
the adaptation module.

666
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:54,420
We will take it
back in simulation.

667
00:28:54,420 --> 00:28:56,280
We will roll it again.

668
00:28:56,280 --> 00:28:59,800
It will hopefully be better than
what it did before, but still

669
00:28:59,800 --> 00:29:00,860
not optimal.

670
00:29:00,860 --> 00:29:04,360
And then we will again supervise
it with the teacher module,

671
00:29:04,360 --> 00:29:08,120
get the data set, run supervised
learning, and then repeat.

672
00:29:08,120 --> 00:29:12,040
So switching to adding vision--

673
00:29:12,040 --> 00:29:14,240
so the most common
approach to add vision

674
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:17,080
is to decouple it
entirely from control

675
00:29:17,080 --> 00:29:20,910
and then build an explicit
map that you can then

676
00:29:20,910 --> 00:29:23,990
feed into your controller
to use that to walk.

677
00:29:23,990 --> 00:29:26,630
And the reason this--
there's a fundamental flaw

678
00:29:26,630 --> 00:29:28,642
in this approach,
which is, first of all,

679
00:29:28,642 --> 00:29:30,350
you're solving a very
hard problem, which

680
00:29:30,350 --> 00:29:32,270
is building an explicit map.

681
00:29:32,270 --> 00:29:33,970
So your map will
always be noisy.

682
00:29:33,970 --> 00:29:36,030
So if you have non-rigid
objects or if you

683
00:29:36,030 --> 00:29:40,250
have drift in estimate of
your pose or any of these,

684
00:29:40,250 --> 00:29:44,310
it will end up converging
to very noisy maps.

685
00:29:44,310 --> 00:29:46,870
So that is a phase where you
have already lost information,

686
00:29:46,870 --> 00:29:49,910
and there is no way you can
recover that information.

687
00:29:49,910 --> 00:29:52,350
And then you feed that
into the controller.

688
00:29:52,350 --> 00:29:54,090
So since we are solving
a harder problem,

689
00:29:54,090 --> 00:29:57,630
we are losing information
that we, in other scenarios,

690
00:29:57,630 --> 00:30:01,590
would not have lost if we were
to directly use the perception

691
00:30:01,590 --> 00:30:04,950
input in the policy.

692
00:30:04,950 --> 00:30:07,990
So here's an example
of how people add noise

693
00:30:07,990 --> 00:30:09,390
to the real terrain.

694
00:30:09,390 --> 00:30:13,770
Maybe the edges were enough to
tell you how to take that step,

695
00:30:13,770 --> 00:30:16,140
but the moment you
convert it to a metric map

696
00:30:16,140 --> 00:30:18,640
and then try to give
that into the policy,

697
00:30:18,640 --> 00:30:20,500
now you need to
train your policy

698
00:30:20,500 --> 00:30:24,780
to be robust to all of these
different kinds of noises, which

699
00:30:24,780 --> 00:30:28,260
is OK for simple scenarios,
but becomes very challenging

700
00:30:28,260 --> 00:30:29,700
the moment you have--

701
00:30:29,700 --> 00:30:32,300
you need to precisely
estimate where the gap is

702
00:30:32,300 --> 00:30:36,060
and avoid stepping on that.

703
00:30:36,060 --> 00:30:39,260
So what we do in this work
is we ask this question,

704
00:30:39,260 --> 00:30:41,320
do we need terrain maps?

705
00:30:41,320 --> 00:30:43,100
Do we need to
explicitly build them?

706
00:30:43,100 --> 00:30:46,260
The answer, we
hypothesize that we don't.

707
00:30:46,260 --> 00:30:49,220
And instead, we directly
couple vision and control

708
00:30:49,220 --> 00:30:50,130
in the final system.

709
00:30:50,130 --> 00:30:52,660

710
00:30:52,660 --> 00:30:55,660
So here is what that system
looks like in deployment.

711
00:30:55,660 --> 00:30:57,160
And I'll get to
how do we train it.

712
00:30:57,160 --> 00:31:01,900
So in the top right is the depth
egocentric that the bot can see.

713
00:31:01,900 --> 00:31:04,300
I've taped the cameras
and compute and everything

714
00:31:04,300 --> 00:31:05,120
on the bot.

715
00:31:05,120 --> 00:31:07,460
So everything is running
on board in real time.

716
00:31:07,460 --> 00:31:09,280
And it's trying to--

717
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:15,420

718
00:31:15,420 --> 00:31:17,760
so it's trying to cross
over these stepping stones.

719
00:31:17,760 --> 00:31:20,340
This is my living
room, and these--

720
00:31:20,340 --> 00:31:24,100
just got 20 stools and then
just got it to walk on it.

721
00:31:24,100 --> 00:31:28,060
And it's like repeated runs
with different levels of gaps

722
00:31:28,060 --> 00:31:29,700
and variations so
that you can test

723
00:31:29,700 --> 00:31:31,500
that it's actually
using perception

724
00:31:31,500 --> 00:31:35,140
to handle all of these.

725
00:31:35,140 --> 00:31:40,420
And here it's some hiking path
where the stairs are uneven.

726
00:31:40,420 --> 00:31:43,200
And the robot is
actually quite small.

727
00:31:43,200 --> 00:31:45,500
So if you give it a
second here-- if you see,

728
00:31:45,500 --> 00:31:48,740
the height of the step is
almost as high as the robot,

729
00:31:48,740 --> 00:31:52,120
and it struggles a bit, but it
manages to climb it at the end.

730
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:55,940

731
00:31:55,940 --> 00:31:59,280
Similarly, first, as we tested
it on uneven stairs uptown,

732
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:01,960
all of that across, and
it was relatively robust.

733
00:32:01,960 --> 00:32:05,140
And if you see how much it
has to get down once it is up,

734
00:32:05,140 --> 00:32:08,420
it is actually quite sharp
for a robot of this size.

735
00:32:08,420 --> 00:32:11,860
And here the ground
is extremely slippery.

736
00:32:11,860 --> 00:32:17,210
There's barely a stable foothold
for the bot, but it keeps trying

737
00:32:17,210 --> 00:32:20,770
and eventually makes it.

738
00:32:20,770 --> 00:32:22,710
Again, here the
step is very high.

739
00:32:22,710 --> 00:32:27,410
It tries a few times
and then it gets it.

740
00:32:27,410 --> 00:32:33,010
One thing, this is a
debris, I think, around CMU.

741
00:32:33,010 --> 00:32:35,890
And one thing to note here
is that it doesn't always

742
00:32:35,890 --> 00:32:37,030
get everything in one shot.

743
00:32:37,030 --> 00:32:39,090
It sometimes needs
to try and retry.

744
00:32:39,090 --> 00:32:41,590
And the retry is make more
sense than the previous try.

745
00:32:41,590 --> 00:32:45,170
That is an example of learning
within the context of what

746
00:32:45,170 --> 00:32:46,610
is fed into the model.

747
00:32:46,610 --> 00:32:51,170
In this one change we make--
and I'll get to that-- is--

748
00:32:51,170 --> 00:32:52,750
So in the first
phase of training,

749
00:32:52,750 --> 00:32:54,210
it's the same as before.

750
00:32:54,210 --> 00:32:57,730
With this additional
terrain height information,

751
00:32:57,730 --> 00:32:58,990
we don't add any noise to it.

752
00:32:58,990 --> 00:33:01,610
This is perfect terrain height
that we feed into the model.

753
00:33:01,610 --> 00:33:05,090
And then we encode all of
that, give it to an MLP

754
00:33:05,090 --> 00:33:08,730
and then train this whole system
with reinforcement learning.

755
00:33:08,730 --> 00:33:11,800
It is much faster to train
because you don't need to render

756
00:33:11,800 --> 00:33:14,520
any perception inputs.

757
00:33:14,520 --> 00:33:16,820
The recipe is the
same as before.

758
00:33:16,820 --> 00:33:18,740
We use PPO in simulation.

759
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:20,240
This time we use IsaacGym.

760
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:22,960
And the rewards are to
track a target velocity

761
00:33:22,960 --> 00:33:25,360
and then minimize energy.

762
00:33:25,360 --> 00:33:27,620
These are the different
training terrains we use.

763
00:33:27,620 --> 00:33:29,728
And we then get a
single policy that

764
00:33:29,728 --> 00:33:31,520
can walk on all of
these different terrains

765
00:33:31,520 --> 00:33:33,240
in simulation.

766
00:33:33,240 --> 00:33:35,100
The problem is same as before.

767
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:37,180
How do we get these
dots in the real world?

768
00:33:37,180 --> 00:33:39,160
And most approaches
try to construct a map

769
00:33:39,160 --> 00:33:40,680
and feed that as input.

770
00:33:40,680 --> 00:33:48,340
But instead, what we try to do
is directly integrate depth.

771
00:33:48,340 --> 00:33:50,240
So we render depth
in simulation.

772
00:33:50,240 --> 00:33:52,920
And then we directly
try to supervise that

773
00:33:52,920 --> 00:33:56,040
with the depth encoder that
was trained in the first phase,

774
00:33:56,040 --> 00:33:58,760
using the same approach as
dagger, where we roll out

775
00:33:58,760 --> 00:34:03,000
the student policy and then we
supervise it with the teacher.

776
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:06,080
And same for the
extrinsic vector.

777
00:34:06,080 --> 00:34:10,909
So here, if you try training
the second phase directly,

778
00:34:10,909 --> 00:34:12,510
it would be an
order of magnitude

779
00:34:12,510 --> 00:34:15,810
slower because rendering
depth takes a lot of time.

780
00:34:15,810 --> 00:34:17,449
So you need to--

781
00:34:17,449 --> 00:34:21,630
this gives you a significant
speedup over how to train it

782
00:34:21,630 --> 00:34:24,550
and lets you deploy
it in the real world

783
00:34:24,550 --> 00:34:28,790
as well, very robustly from
what I just showed you.

784
00:34:28,790 --> 00:34:30,770
So this is the final
deployment policy.

785
00:34:30,770 --> 00:34:32,710
It takes the egocentric
depth from the camera

786
00:34:32,710 --> 00:34:36,170
that's on the robot and the
full proprioceptive history.

787
00:34:36,170 --> 00:34:39,949
And the one change that we've
made here is the use of RNNs.

788
00:34:39,949 --> 00:34:43,409
So we use RNNs so they can give
you a slightly longer context.

789
00:34:43,409 --> 00:34:45,070
So RNNs are Recurrent
Neural Networks

790
00:34:45,070 --> 00:34:47,750
which integrate
information over time.

791
00:34:47,750 --> 00:34:50,989
But they're not known to
preserve very long horizon

792
00:34:50,989 --> 00:34:54,190
information, which is
why most of the community

793
00:34:54,190 --> 00:34:57,070
moved to transformers when they
wanted to do very long sequence

794
00:34:57,070 --> 00:34:57,830
modeling.

795
00:34:57,830 --> 00:35:02,570
We used RNN, which gives a good
enough context to the model,

796
00:35:02,570 --> 00:35:06,670
but it's-- we did not ablate
using a transformer instead

797
00:35:06,670 --> 00:35:10,860
for this task to see if that
improves performance or not.

798
00:35:10,860 --> 00:35:14,660
So now when we
deploy this policy--

799
00:35:14,660 --> 00:35:17,800
to point this out again, I think
stairs are designed for humans.

800
00:35:17,800 --> 00:35:20,460
And this robot is
extremely short.

801
00:35:20,460 --> 00:35:23,360
So relative to the height of the
robot, these stairs are tall.

802
00:35:23,360 --> 00:35:27,780
So there isn't enough
clearance under the robot

803
00:35:27,780 --> 00:35:31,380
to just raise its leg
and then take the step.

804
00:35:31,380 --> 00:35:33,100
Also, when it is
going down, if it just

805
00:35:33,100 --> 00:35:36,180
does it in a way which
everyone else would,

806
00:35:36,180 --> 00:35:39,018
the amount of tilt you
would see in a spot robot

807
00:35:39,018 --> 00:35:41,060
would be much smaller,
because it's a much taller

808
00:35:41,060 --> 00:35:44,620
robot while going down,
whereas for a small robot,

809
00:35:44,620 --> 00:35:46,460
it needs to use
majority of its leg.

810
00:35:46,460 --> 00:35:48,180
And the discrepancy
will be much higher

811
00:35:48,180 --> 00:35:50,140
just because the
legs are smaller.

812
00:35:50,140 --> 00:35:54,260
So what it learns is it learns
to move the leg out sideways

813
00:35:54,260 --> 00:35:56,158
to climb all these stairs.

814
00:35:56,158 --> 00:35:57,700
And this is possible
because we don't

815
00:35:57,700 --> 00:36:01,200
use any predefined gait or
foot motions or any of that.

816
00:36:01,200 --> 00:36:03,500
And we let it be learned
using reinforcement

817
00:36:03,500 --> 00:36:04,990
learning through search.

818
00:36:04,990 --> 00:36:10,100

819
00:36:10,100 --> 00:36:12,320
And this footstep
planning is also emergent.

820
00:36:12,320 --> 00:36:14,740
So it only takes a
step when it needs to.

821
00:36:14,740 --> 00:36:17,300
It doesn't constantly
move its feet

822
00:36:17,300 --> 00:36:19,800
to if we had a predefined
gait of any sort.

823
00:36:19,800 --> 00:36:22,220
And we see that it's
actually quite smooth

824
00:36:22,220 --> 00:36:24,800
and seems to walk with
intention and precision,

825
00:36:24,800 --> 00:36:28,370
and it can handle these kinds
of scenarios very easily.

826
00:36:28,370 --> 00:36:37,860

827
00:36:37,860 --> 00:36:40,180
And then, again,
a same policy can

828
00:36:40,180 --> 00:36:42,780
control for all of these
different variations

829
00:36:42,780 --> 00:36:43,720
of environments.

830
00:36:43,720 --> 00:36:46,000
We don't use any explicit
maps, no explicit gaits.

831
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:50,140
And that is critical to get the
results we do in this scenario.

832
00:36:50,140 --> 00:36:55,740
We also gave a live
demo at multiple places,

833
00:36:55,740 --> 00:36:58,320
and it was quite robust in
a lot of these scenarios.

834
00:36:58,320 --> 00:37:01,060
And some of these demos,
I think at the bottom one,

835
00:37:01,060 --> 00:37:06,040
the right foot padding just
got out and started limping,

836
00:37:06,040 --> 00:37:07,830
but it continued to
do the full demo.

837
00:37:07,830 --> 00:37:11,050

838
00:37:11,050 --> 00:37:13,350
So let's quickly compare
this with baseline.

839
00:37:13,350 --> 00:37:14,990
I have three key baselines.

840
00:37:14,990 --> 00:37:17,070
The first is just
having a blind policy,

841
00:37:17,070 --> 00:37:18,810
so the same as
what we had before.

842
00:37:18,810 --> 00:37:21,610
The second one uses
a map and then feeds

843
00:37:21,610 --> 00:37:25,450
a slightly noisy version of
the map to see how it works.

844
00:37:25,450 --> 00:37:27,530
It's also trained
with that noise.

845
00:37:27,530 --> 00:37:30,770
And then the last is
what I just demonstrated

846
00:37:30,770 --> 00:37:32,410
directly going from depth.

847
00:37:32,410 --> 00:37:34,810
So we see that the
gap is not that much

848
00:37:34,810 --> 00:37:37,650
for simple terrains
like a slope,

849
00:37:37,650 --> 00:37:41,650
but the moment we get to more
challenging terrains, a map

850
00:37:41,650 --> 00:37:43,612
based method is almost
as bad as blind,

851
00:37:43,612 --> 00:37:45,570
because if we sample from
the same distribution

852
00:37:45,570 --> 00:37:48,490
that other works have done, we
see that the same performance

853
00:37:48,490 --> 00:37:50,130
goes down significantly.

854
00:37:50,130 --> 00:37:53,610
So you need the
final action head

855
00:37:53,610 --> 00:37:56,910
to know whatever it needs
to from perception directly

856
00:37:56,910 --> 00:37:59,170
without adding any
noise or any bottlenecks

857
00:37:59,170 --> 00:38:02,610
for it to work well
in this scenario.

858
00:38:02,610 --> 00:38:05,027
Similarly, for stairs
and discrete obstacles,

859
00:38:05,027 --> 00:38:07,360
we see that the performance
is better than the other two

860
00:38:07,360 --> 00:38:07,860
methods.

861
00:38:07,860 --> 00:38:13,240
But the other methods still
fall behind the performance

862
00:38:13,240 --> 00:38:14,200
of this method.

863
00:38:14,200 --> 00:38:16,480
One interesting
thing is, for stairs,

864
00:38:16,480 --> 00:38:21,760
it's better to be blind and just
learn to put your steps blindly

865
00:38:21,760 --> 00:38:24,680
than to use a map which is
noisy and which gives you

866
00:38:24,680 --> 00:38:26,320
an inaccurate
estimate of where you

867
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:27,700
might have to place your foot.

868
00:38:27,700 --> 00:38:30,840

869
00:38:30,840 --> 00:38:32,890
Up until now, I've
developed it for locomotion.

870
00:38:32,890 --> 00:38:34,640
And then the same thing
is applicable also

871
00:38:34,640 --> 00:38:38,480
to one scenario in
dexterous manipulation.

872
00:38:38,480 --> 00:38:42,780
So this is the task of just
rotating objects in hand.

873
00:38:42,780 --> 00:38:46,305
It's the same policy, and
it's capable of handling

874
00:38:46,305 --> 00:38:48,180
all of these different
variations of objects.

875
00:38:48,180 --> 00:38:49,763
There's a paper towel
and then there's

876
00:38:49,763 --> 00:38:55,080
a badminton shuttle and all
of these things in this.

877
00:38:55,080 --> 00:38:56,600
And it is capable
of generalizing

878
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:58,820
to objects with very
different physical properties.

879
00:38:58,820 --> 00:39:01,440
So the weight
variation can be huge,

880
00:39:01,440 --> 00:39:05,230
from 5 grams to
almost 200 grams.

881
00:39:05,230 --> 00:39:07,710
The size-- the coefficient of
friction-- so this is an ice

882
00:39:07,710 --> 00:39:09,710
ball, and on the
right is a kiwi, which

883
00:39:09,710 --> 00:39:13,430
has a slightly rough surface.

884
00:39:13,430 --> 00:39:16,510
The center of mass
on the right is

885
00:39:16,510 --> 00:39:19,210
below the finger because of
the weight that hangs from it,

886
00:39:19,210 --> 00:39:22,150
and on the left it's
slightly above the finger.

887
00:39:22,150 --> 00:39:24,380
And it seems to do fine.

888
00:39:24,380 --> 00:39:26,130
And here the shape is
also very different.

889
00:39:26,130 --> 00:39:27,630
It could be a cube or a cup.

890
00:39:27,630 --> 00:39:29,628
And it performs fine still.

891
00:39:29,628 --> 00:39:31,670
And this was also trained
in simulation, deployed

892
00:39:31,670 --> 00:39:34,950
in the real world 0 shot.

893
00:39:34,950 --> 00:39:36,450
And the key idea
is the same here.

894
00:39:36,450 --> 00:39:40,932
We use proprioceptive history
to give the model as an input,

895
00:39:40,932 --> 00:39:42,390
and then it uses
that to figure out

896
00:39:42,390 --> 00:39:44,790
how to learn a general model.

897
00:39:44,790 --> 00:39:48,630
One hypothesis, since we don't
know exactly what the model is

898
00:39:48,630 --> 00:39:52,190
doing, is it uses proprioceptive
history for contact detection.

899
00:39:52,190 --> 00:39:53,390
So if you look at this--

900
00:39:53,390 --> 00:39:54,890
and if you look at
the bottom joint,

901
00:39:54,890 --> 00:39:55,850
that's what we have plotted.

902
00:39:55,850 --> 00:39:57,308
So we have the
actions, and then we

903
00:39:57,308 --> 00:40:00,670
have the joint position,
which is actually achieved.

904
00:40:00,670 --> 00:40:04,140
And if you look at the time when
it makes the contact or not,

905
00:40:04,140 --> 00:40:06,300
it turns out that the
time when it is not

906
00:40:06,300 --> 00:40:09,360
in contact correlates with
the bottom part of the graph,

907
00:40:09,360 --> 00:40:11,880
where they do overlap
with each other.

908
00:40:11,880 --> 00:40:13,620
And the time when
it does make contact

909
00:40:13,620 --> 00:40:16,560
is at the top of the graph,
where it's in contact,

910
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:18,460
and then it's not able
to achieve the desired

911
00:40:18,460 --> 00:40:21,065
joint position that
it is commanding.

912
00:40:21,065 --> 00:40:23,440
And this is enough signal for
the network to estimate it.

913
00:40:23,440 --> 00:40:25,500
And if you can
estimate contacts,

914
00:40:25,500 --> 00:40:30,500
you can figure out the shape
and size of the object.

915
00:40:30,500 --> 00:40:32,940
That helps you in
adapting to all

916
00:40:32,940 --> 00:40:36,140
of these different variations.

917
00:40:36,140 --> 00:40:38,760
We did the same, gave live
demos for these as well.

918
00:40:38,760 --> 00:40:40,940
So in the center one,
this is an orange peel.

919
00:40:40,940 --> 00:40:43,900
So if you apply a slightly
larger force than necessary,

920
00:40:43,900 --> 00:40:45,600
it will completely deform.

921
00:40:45,600 --> 00:40:48,948
And on the right is an adapter.

922
00:40:48,948 --> 00:40:51,240
This has not been seen in
training data and simulation.

923
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:56,540
We don't simulate objects to
this level of granularity.

924
00:40:56,540 --> 00:41:00,610
And again, the same thing can be
applied to drone flight as well.

925
00:41:00,610 --> 00:41:03,010
So we have the same policy,
controlling the drone

926
00:41:03,010 --> 00:41:05,970
at the top and the bottom,
so the same weights, which

927
00:41:05,970 --> 00:41:08,650
tries to estimate the mass and
arm length, and all of these,

928
00:41:08,650 --> 00:41:10,150
of the drones.

929
00:41:10,150 --> 00:41:12,013
The top drone is
four times heavier

930
00:41:12,013 --> 00:41:13,930
than the one at the
bottom, and the arm length

931
00:41:13,930 --> 00:41:16,730
is also three times.

932
00:41:16,730 --> 00:41:20,290
And the same weight is able to
estimate what morphology it's

933
00:41:20,290 --> 00:41:23,250
operating in, and it's able
to adapt and fly the drones.

934
00:41:23,250 --> 00:41:25,730
It's directly controlling
the motor velocity

935
00:41:25,730 --> 00:41:28,170
of each of the propellers.

936
00:41:28,170 --> 00:41:30,010
So it's not abstracting
away to a level

937
00:41:30,010 --> 00:41:32,370
where this becomes
an easy problem.

938
00:41:32,370 --> 00:41:33,550
And it's also very robust.

939
00:41:33,550 --> 00:41:36,050
So this is another drone
with a higher mass.

940
00:41:36,050 --> 00:41:39,432

941
00:41:39,432 --> 00:41:40,890
Initially, it
wobbles a bit I think

942
00:41:40,890 --> 00:41:45,010
it's still trying to estimate
what drone it is flying.

943
00:41:45,010 --> 00:41:47,210
And then we'll
take a tennis ball

944
00:41:47,210 --> 00:41:50,330
and shoot it at the
[? fence. ?] So these are

945
00:41:50,330 --> 00:41:52,150
the propellers of the drone.

946
00:41:52,150 --> 00:41:54,490
And then we'll shoot it
at one of the propellers.

947
00:41:54,490 --> 00:41:58,030
And it's still
quite robust to it.

948
00:41:58,030 --> 00:42:02,290

949
00:42:02,290 --> 00:42:05,793
So I have shown all
of these results.

950
00:42:05,793 --> 00:42:07,210
And then the next
natural question

951
00:42:07,210 --> 00:42:10,050
is, what is there left to solve?

952
00:42:10,050 --> 00:42:11,450
Well, I think almost everything.

953
00:42:11,450 --> 00:42:14,890
We still don't have a
general purpose robot

954
00:42:14,890 --> 00:42:18,450
model that can control a general
purpose hardware to achieve

955
00:42:18,450 --> 00:42:21,410
the task that humans can.

956
00:42:21,410 --> 00:42:26,090
So I'll focus on humanoids as
the general form factor, which

957
00:42:26,090 --> 00:42:28,850
we know can achieve every
single thing that humans can,

958
00:42:28,850 --> 00:42:33,330
and show you the progress that
we have made at Tesla on this.

959
00:42:33,330 --> 00:42:35,390
So you might have seen
some of these videos.

960
00:42:35,390 --> 00:42:38,370
On the left is a biped
walking on a hill.

961
00:42:38,370 --> 00:42:40,690
I've showed similar
results with a quadruped,

962
00:42:40,690 --> 00:42:43,330
but a biped is much less
forgiving because it's

963
00:42:43,330 --> 00:42:44,990
a much more unstable system.

964
00:42:44,990 --> 00:42:47,250
And you need to
be more reactive.

965
00:42:47,250 --> 00:42:49,430
All of these results are
also trained in simulation,

966
00:42:49,430 --> 00:42:51,210
deployed 0 shot on hardware.

967
00:42:51,210 --> 00:42:54,890
And then a more recent
video of the bot dancing,

968
00:42:54,890 --> 00:42:57,880
all real-time on
board autonomous.

969
00:42:57,880 --> 00:43:01,560
Getting the first thing to work
and the 10th thing to work does

970
00:43:01,560 --> 00:43:03,865
not take very long because
the pipeline is very simple.

971
00:43:03,865 --> 00:43:06,240
Once you get one thing to
work, you can actually pump out

972
00:43:06,240 --> 00:43:08,920
a lot more results very easily.

973
00:43:08,920 --> 00:43:13,380
And then this is the progress
we've made on manipulation.

974
00:43:13,380 --> 00:43:15,720
So this is the
same neural network

975
00:43:15,720 --> 00:43:20,360
that takes language as input
and then can execute whatever

976
00:43:20,360 --> 00:43:22,280
the language command is.

977
00:43:22,280 --> 00:43:26,440
Here the key ingredient
is the use of videos.

978
00:43:26,440 --> 00:43:31,200
We learned this from videos
and a little bit of robot data.

979
00:43:31,200 --> 00:43:32,705
And we're able to get this.

980
00:43:32,705 --> 00:43:34,080
This is the progress
that we have

981
00:43:34,080 --> 00:43:36,440
up until now in manipulation.

982
00:43:36,440 --> 00:43:38,440
Again, this is the
same policy, doing

983
00:43:38,440 --> 00:43:41,080
all of these things
conditioned on language.

984
00:43:41,080 --> 00:43:44,300
And we can get decent
performance on these tasks,

985
00:43:44,300 --> 00:43:46,400
but they're not very reliable.

986
00:43:46,400 --> 00:43:48,800
This was learned using
egocentric videos

987
00:43:48,800 --> 00:43:50,260
of humans doing things.

988
00:43:50,260 --> 00:43:53,880
And then we translated that
somehow to the robot results

989
00:43:53,880 --> 00:43:55,560
that I've shown you.

990
00:43:55,560 --> 00:43:58,430
So these results are
not very reliable.

991
00:43:58,430 --> 00:44:01,790
And where we eventually
want to get to

992
00:44:01,790 --> 00:44:05,610
is reliability and dexterity to
the level of what humans have.

993
00:44:05,610 --> 00:44:08,110
This is the eventual goal
where we want to get to.

994
00:44:08,110 --> 00:44:11,290
So we want consistency,
dexterity, agility,

995
00:44:11,290 --> 00:44:15,150
and reliability in the
final system that we have.

996
00:44:15,150 --> 00:44:17,590
So if we take a step
back in all the results

997
00:44:17,590 --> 00:44:20,390
that I presented
for locomotion--

998
00:44:20,390 --> 00:44:23,310
the results that we got were
very agile, dexterous, and very

999
00:44:23,310 --> 00:44:25,470
reliable in the real world.

1000
00:44:25,470 --> 00:44:27,330
And those results,
like I mentioned,

1001
00:44:27,330 --> 00:44:29,190
were three to four years ago.

1002
00:44:29,190 --> 00:44:31,350
So why do we not
already have some

1003
00:44:31,350 --> 00:44:34,110
or most of these results for
manipulation in the real world

1004
00:44:34,110 --> 00:44:37,310
to the same level of
dexterity and reliability?

1005
00:44:37,310 --> 00:44:41,030
And if you go back to the two
things which got locomotion

1006
00:44:41,030 --> 00:44:44,730
to work well, one was-- the
simulation technology was ready.

1007
00:44:44,730 --> 00:44:46,870
Rigid body contact
was good enough

1008
00:44:46,870 --> 00:44:48,870
to get very robust locomotion.

1009
00:44:48,870 --> 00:44:50,290
But when it comes
to manipulation,

1010
00:44:50,290 --> 00:44:52,610
and if you have to
chop vegetables,

1011
00:44:52,610 --> 00:44:54,500
we need to simulate that.

1012
00:44:54,500 --> 00:44:56,180
We need to simulate
that, and then we

1013
00:44:56,180 --> 00:44:58,860
need to simulate deformable
objects, dough and everything

1014
00:44:58,860 --> 00:45:01,620
in simulation to get the
sim-to-real transfer to work

1015
00:45:01,620 --> 00:45:03,300
for this.

1016
00:45:03,300 --> 00:45:04,860
And then the second
comparison are

1017
00:45:04,860 --> 00:45:08,300
rewards, which are much easier
to specify for locomotion.

1018
00:45:08,300 --> 00:45:10,460
So if you
programmatically write out

1019
00:45:10,460 --> 00:45:13,780
a reward which asks you to
track a certain velocity,

1020
00:45:13,780 --> 00:45:15,540
it's complete.

1021
00:45:15,540 --> 00:45:17,508
This is the full
task specification.

1022
00:45:17,508 --> 00:45:19,300
And now you can just
randomize the terrain,

1023
00:45:19,300 --> 00:45:21,660
and then you can get a
general policy out of it.

1024
00:45:21,660 --> 00:45:23,720
But that's not true
for manipulation.

1025
00:45:23,720 --> 00:45:27,100
So for manipulation, if
you see, depending on--

1026
00:45:27,100 --> 00:45:29,180
if you want to chop
a vegetable, and even

1027
00:45:29,180 --> 00:45:33,345
if you had this simulated
and full state of simulation,

1028
00:45:33,345 --> 00:45:35,220
you would still need to
come up with a reward

1029
00:45:35,220 --> 00:45:37,500
which measures the size
of each of the slices

1030
00:45:37,500 --> 00:45:40,860
that are cut, and so on, and
that itself will depend on

1031
00:45:40,860 --> 00:45:43,660
what exactly is it that you're
trying to cook eventually.

1032
00:45:43,660 --> 00:45:45,285
So that's one reward.

1033
00:45:45,285 --> 00:45:46,660
And let's say you
put in the work

1034
00:45:46,660 --> 00:45:49,860
to do that reward, that
only gets you one task.

1035
00:45:49,860 --> 00:45:52,520
It doesn't get you the full
generality of manipulation.

1036
00:45:52,520 --> 00:45:54,570
So for example, if you
want to stretch the dough

1037
00:45:54,570 --> 00:45:55,930
or put flour on
the dough, you need

1038
00:45:55,930 --> 00:45:57,222
to come up with another reward.

1039
00:45:57,222 --> 00:45:58,990
So it's a very manual process.

1040
00:45:58,990 --> 00:46:00,890
And even if you get
one of this right,

1041
00:46:00,890 --> 00:46:04,090
it will not scale
to anything else.

1042
00:46:04,090 --> 00:46:09,170
So everything I'll present
from now on is speculative.

1043
00:46:09,170 --> 00:46:12,650
So let's start talking
about simulation.

1044
00:46:12,650 --> 00:46:14,870
For some scenarios,
like deformable objects,

1045
00:46:14,870 --> 00:46:16,590
it is true that we
can simulate it,

1046
00:46:16,590 --> 00:46:18,950
except that we cannot
simulate it very fast.

1047
00:46:18,950 --> 00:46:21,450
And then there are some other
more complex interactions that

1048
00:46:21,450 --> 00:46:23,490
we cannot simulate at all.

1049
00:46:23,490 --> 00:46:28,290
But there's no obvious
fundamental reason why we cannot

1050
00:46:28,290 --> 00:46:33,010
simulate more complex scenarios
as we get more compute.

1051
00:46:33,010 --> 00:46:36,930
So if we bet on
compute, then the hope

1052
00:46:36,930 --> 00:46:39,170
is as compute
progresses, simulation

1053
00:46:39,170 --> 00:46:42,170
can progress and can give you
progressively better results

1054
00:46:42,170 --> 00:46:45,050
in the real world as we expand
the scope of what simulation

1055
00:46:45,050 --> 00:46:46,010
can do.

1056
00:46:46,010 --> 00:46:49,430
And if we are to learn
from the bitter lesson,

1057
00:46:49,430 --> 00:46:51,090
then compute would
be the right bet

1058
00:46:51,090 --> 00:46:53,440
to take here, which is you
just hope that simulation

1059
00:46:53,440 --> 00:46:54,960
can improve over time.

1060
00:46:54,960 --> 00:46:57,780
For those of you not familiar
with the bitter lesson,

1061
00:46:57,780 --> 00:47:00,360
it's a very short
piece by Rich Sutton.

1062
00:47:00,360 --> 00:47:03,360
I highly recommend
everyone to read it.

1063
00:47:03,360 --> 00:47:05,260
I just have the
first line from it,

1064
00:47:05,260 --> 00:47:09,840
which says that general methods
that leverage compute are

1065
00:47:09,840 --> 00:47:12,090
ultimately the most effective
and by a large margin.

1066
00:47:12,090 --> 00:47:14,720

1067
00:47:14,720 --> 00:47:18,920
Switching to the other topic
of having a very general reward

1068
00:47:18,920 --> 00:47:20,280
model--

1069
00:47:20,280 --> 00:47:22,160
the most obvious thing
that comes to mind

1070
00:47:22,160 --> 00:47:24,920
is, can we train a
general, large, big model

1071
00:47:24,920 --> 00:47:27,040
for general understanding
of the world

1072
00:47:27,040 --> 00:47:30,040
and somehow use that to
define the rewards that we

1073
00:47:30,040 --> 00:47:33,320
want to improve our policies?

1074
00:47:33,320 --> 00:47:37,332
This could work,
but RL, as we know,

1075
00:47:37,332 --> 00:47:40,660
has exploited weaknesses
in the model if they exist.

1076
00:47:40,660 --> 00:47:43,840
So when we do RLHF, if we
continue tuning with RLHF,

1077
00:47:43,840 --> 00:47:47,520
we see that the model goes
completely out of distribution

1078
00:47:47,520 --> 00:47:49,480
from what you would
like it to do.

1079
00:47:49,480 --> 00:47:51,500
Same with the DeepSeek paper.

1080
00:47:51,500 --> 00:47:54,400
They find that if you don't
use verifiable rewards

1081
00:47:54,400 --> 00:47:56,840
or rule-based rewards like
they do, at some point

1082
00:47:56,840 --> 00:47:58,880
RL can start
exploiting the rewards

1083
00:47:58,880 --> 00:48:02,040
that you have defined for them.

1084
00:48:02,040 --> 00:48:04,640
So I was optimistic
about simulation.

1085
00:48:04,640 --> 00:48:07,560
Why am I optimistic
about this general reward

1086
00:48:07,560 --> 00:48:13,360
model, which also seems to be
a very hard problem to solve?

1087
00:48:13,360 --> 00:48:16,400
The reason I'm optimistic is
because the absolute worst

1088
00:48:16,400 --> 00:48:20,800
case is we get humans to
label all the rewards for us,

1089
00:48:20,800 --> 00:48:22,920
and humans are very
scalable because we

1090
00:48:22,920 --> 00:48:26,320
have billions of them.

1091
00:48:26,320 --> 00:48:28,600
And then before I
end this talk, I

1092
00:48:28,600 --> 00:48:31,360
would like to just
revisit how we

1093
00:48:31,360 --> 00:48:34,720
approach the idea of robotics.

1094
00:48:34,720 --> 00:48:37,400
So most of the
definition that I've

1095
00:48:37,400 --> 00:48:40,040
used during the course of the
talk, and most work in robot

1096
00:48:40,040 --> 00:48:42,120
learning, tries to
approach robotics

1097
00:48:42,120 --> 00:48:44,400
as achieving parity with human.

1098
00:48:44,400 --> 00:48:46,120
The day we achieve
that, it will be

1099
00:48:46,120 --> 00:48:49,030
a civilization-changing event.

1100
00:48:49,030 --> 00:48:52,030
However, that will just be the
first step in everything that we

1101
00:48:52,030 --> 00:48:55,390
can achieve from robotics,
because the actuators that we

1102
00:48:55,390 --> 00:49:00,750
make driven by electrical torque
and the computers which are

1103
00:49:00,750 --> 00:49:03,550
based on silicon as opposed
to carbon than what we have,

1104
00:49:03,550 --> 00:49:06,990
it is possible that they can
exploit all of that to achieve

1105
00:49:06,990 --> 00:49:11,470
capabilities which humans have
not thought of or cannot figure

1106
00:49:11,470 --> 00:49:12,390
out how to do.

1107
00:49:12,390 --> 00:49:14,170
So they could do
things much faster,

1108
00:49:14,170 --> 00:49:17,430
more efficiently, or better, or
can be much more agile in extras

1109
00:49:17,430 --> 00:49:19,030
than what humans are.

1110
00:49:19,030 --> 00:49:21,150
And then one system that
we know which has already

1111
00:49:21,150 --> 00:49:24,950
done it is AlphaGo, where the
key was to use a sparse reward,

1112
00:49:24,950 --> 00:49:27,110
and then lots and lots of
search or reinforcement

1113
00:49:27,110 --> 00:49:29,870
learning to get it to discover
new behaviors which are

1114
00:49:29,870 --> 00:49:34,190
beyond what humans could think.

1115
00:49:34,190 --> 00:49:38,290
So this is, I think, a more
general or a follow-up question.

1116
00:49:38,290 --> 00:49:41,130
Can we get robots to not
just match human capability,

1117
00:49:41,130 --> 00:49:43,940
but exceed what humans can do?

1118
00:49:43,940 --> 00:49:48,000

